<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Layla and Majnun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1188">1188</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Layla and Majnun is a Persian narrative poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a>, completed in 1188 at the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhsitan-i">Akhsitan I</a>. Based on an Arabic tale of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>, driven mad by his hopeless love for Layla, it became one of the best-known love stories of the Islamic world. It forms part of Nizami's Khamsa and inspired many later versions in Persian, Turkic and other languages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun.jpg" alt="Layla and Majnun" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Layla_and_Majnun2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The story belongs first to Arabic tradition. It concerns the 7th-century poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> ibn al-Mulawwah and Layla al-Amiriyah, and it originates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>, in present-day Saudi Arabia, where the two are traditionally associated with Al-Aflaj and with a town named after Layla. Arabic records place Qays's birth around 645 and his death around 688, in the reign of the Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a> the legend circulated in short anecdotes, recorded in the Kitab al-Aghani and in Ibn Qutaybah's Al-Shi'r wa-l-Shu'ara', which are loosely connected and show little plot development. Nizami gathered both secular and mystical sources and drew on Udhrite love poetry, which is marked by longing for a beloved who cannot be had.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In the older account <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> and Layla fell in love when young. He composed poems naming her often, and his obsession led his tribe, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-amir">Banu Amir</a>, to call him Majnun, meaning crazy. Her father refused his request, since it would be a scandal for Layla to marry a man thought unbalanced, and she was forcibly married to a rich merchant of the Thaqif tribe, Ward al-Thaqafi. Majnun left the camp and wandered the desert, reciting poetry to himself, while his family left food for him. Layla is generally said to have moved north with her husband, fallen ill and died. Majnun was later found dead near her grave, where he had carved three verses on a rock.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a>'s version the lovers meet at school, but a family feud keeps them apart and Layla's family arranges her marriage to another man. The love is called virgin love, because the lovers never marry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a>'s poem, composed in 1188 as the third part of his Khamsa, carried the theme from Arabic into Persian, Turkish and Indic languages. Imitations followed, among them Amir Khusrow Dehlavi's Majnun o Leyli, completed in 1299, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a>'s version of 1484, which runs to 3,860 couplets. Fuzuli's poem in Azerbaijani, written in 1535, became widely known in Ottoman Turkey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The scholar Hekmat listed forty Persian and thirteen Turkish versions. Mystics told many stories of Majnun to illustrate concepts such as fanaa, annihilation, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> writing the name Layla stands for the Beloved.</p><p>Sir William Jones published Hatefi's version in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1788, and Isaac D'Israeli translated the poem into English early in the 19th century. In 1908 the Azerbaijani composer Uzeyir Hajibeyov based the first opera of the Islamic world, Leyli and Majnun, on Fuzuli's work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Layla and Majnun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nizami Ganjavi’s <b>Layla and Majnun</b>, one of the most retold love stories of the Islamic world, was composed in 1188 at the request of Shirvanshah Akhsitan I.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…a great mathematician and astronomer, wrote quatrains on the brevity of life; Nizami wrote romantic epics such as <b>Layla and Majnun</b> and Khosrow and Shirin; Attar wrote the Conference of the Birds, an allegory of the mystical path; Saadi wrote the Gulistan, “The Rose Garden”, in prose and…</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
